  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">NIHR HPSR-CA - Reducing delays in cancer care in sub-Saharan Africa</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">A UK and lower- and middle-income country (LMIC) partnership (University of Ibadan, Bayero University, African Population and Health Research Center, Kenya Medical Research Institute, University of Birmingham, University of Edinburgh, University of Warwick, University of Exeter, Georgetown University) that aims to develop and then evaluate a service change to avoid delays in receiving cancer care. This study will take place over 54 months from November 2024 to April 2029. Cancer is the second largest cause of death in sub-Saharan Africa (SSA) and the outlook for cancer is much worse in SSA than in high-income countries, mostly because people already have late-stage, and therefore incurable, disease by the time they are diagnosed. Approximately half of the delay arises after the person has been to a clinic, and it is this delay within the health sector that we will study.

We will build on our ongoing relationships with local governments in two countries: Oyo and Kano states in Nigeria, and Nairobi County in Kenya. These countries are increasingly using public messaging to encourage people with serious symptoms to attend health clinics, and they are upgrading specialist services. It is therefore crucial to close the gap and provide an effective, efficient, compassionate, and equitable pathway from presentation at a primary health clinic to receiving specialist treatment. We will study care for people with symptoms of seven &#x2018;marker&#x2019; cancers; uterus (womb), colon, breast, stomach and gullet (oesophageal), lung, prostate/bladder/kidney, and head/neck. Working with patients, policymakers and civil society we will design interventions to reduce delay arising in primary health clinics by designing an educational programme and to reduce delays after referral by developing a programme of peer navigation assistance.

To evaluate clinician education we will test knowledge through structured tests and the use of &apos;mystery shoppers&apos; who will go into clinics to pose as patients with possible cancer symptoms. We will evaluate both interventions by monitoring referral rates, patient wellbeing and delay. To better understand factors that influence the successes of our interventions, we will study the broader system, including workforce, collaboration between organisations, leadership and governance.

Community members and policymakers will work with us to disseminate findings and engage people across SSA and beyond so that they can learn from what we have done and advocate for similar services locally.</narrative>
  </description>
